<h2 id="idea">1. The idea in one paragraph</h2>
<p>Two Claude sessions each get their own Git worktree at the same pinned commit and a task file. A human types in those terminals. When the human decides both are done, the controller <em>freezes</em>: it stops the sessions, captures each worktree into an immutable snapshot commit, and checks that each worker only touched paths it owns. Each snapshot is then verified in a fresh worktree by running the policy's build, unit, and Playwright checks, and the combined candidate (base + both snapshots) is verified again. A human-supplied independent review and a separate approval, both bound to the exact hash of the evidence bundle, unlock a local fast-forward of the feature branch. Nothing is pushed. Every step is a LangGraph node whose progress is checkpointed to SQLite, so any CLI invocation can reopen the run and continue from where it stopped.</p>

<h2 id="modules">2. Modules and who calls whom</h2>
<p>All code lives in <code>workflow/</code>. <code>pipeline.py</code> is the hub: it owns the <code>Pipeline</code> runtime class, builds the graph, and hosts the CLI. Everything else is a helper it calls. The older modules <code>graph.py</code>, <code>demo.py</code>, <code>live.py</code>, and <code>observer.py</code> are earlier slices kept as examples; the production path never imports <code>graph.py</code> or <code>live.py</code>.</p>

