<p>The state type is <code>PipelineState</code>, a <code>TypedDict</code> with <code>total=False</code>. Because keys are optional and each node returns only its own keys, LangGraph merges them by simple assignment, and the presence of a key doubles as "that node completed". This is exactly the rule the feature README gives the future viewer: <code>launch_ui</code> is done when <code>values.ui</code> exists, and so on.</p>

<h2 id="langgraph">4. LangGraph concepts the code relies on</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>StateGraph and edges.</strong> <code>add_edge(START, "launch_ui")</code> and <code>add_edge(START, "launch_adapter")</code> fan out; <code>add_edge(["launch_ui","launch_adapter"], "handoff")</code> is a join that waits for both. Same pattern for the two verify lanes into <code>candidate</code>.</li>
<li><strong>Supersteps.</strong> All nodes that are ready run together as one step. If any node in a step raises, the step fails, but LangGraph still saves the successful siblings' pending writes to the checkpoint. This is what makes "retry only the failed lane" work without special code.</li>
<li><strong>Checkpointer and thread.</strong> <code>SqliteSaver.from_conn_string(run/pipeline.sqlite)</code> with <code>thread_id = run_id</code>. Every CLI process opens the same file and the same thread, so <code>graph.get_state(config)</code> returns exactly where the previous process stopped.</li>
<li><strong>interrupt() and Command(resume=…).</strong> Calling <code>interrupt(payload)</code> inside a node stops the graph and stores the payload. <code>graph.invoke(Command(resume=value), config)</code> reruns that node from the top; this time <code>interrupt()</code> returns <code>value</code> instead of stopping. That is why the handoff node does no work before the interrupt and all its work after it.</li>
<li><strong>invoke(None).</strong> Invoking with no input and no resume simply continues from the checkpoint. The <code>retry</code> and <code>reconcile</code> actions use this.</li>
<li><strong>Reading state.</strong> <code>state.next</code> lists node names that are pending; <code>state.tasks</code> carries each pending task's <code>error</code> and <code>interrupts</code>. The CLI derives <code>pending = [interrupt kinds]</code> from that and gates every action on it.</li>
</ul>

<div class="callout">
<p><strong>Idempotency is the runtime's job, not LangGraph's.</strong> A resumed node reruns from its first line. So <code>freeze()</code> returns early if <code>snapshots.json</code> exists, <code>verify_revision()</code> returns the existing packet if one is on disk for that attempt, <code>candidate()</code> reuses <code>candidate.json</code>, and <code>integrate()</code> checks <code>integration-intent.json</code> before touching Git. Every node that has an external side effect journals its intent to a file first.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="lifecycle">5. One run, command by command</h2>
<p>The one-command launcher performs the first four steps for you; the rest are the operator's. Each item lists what runs, and which files in the run directory appear.</p>

<ol class="steps">
<li><h4>preflight <span class="pill auto">no agents</span></h4>
<p>Validates the policy schema and the fixed <code>ui/frontend</code>, <code>adapter/backend</code> role pairing, requires a clean source checkout, checks that <code>git</code>, <code>claude</code>, <code>node</code> exist, that the Claude CLI has the <code>--bg --safe-mode --tools --permission-mode</code> flags, that <code>claude auth status</code> reports logged in, and with <code>--herdr</code> that <code>HERDR_ENV=1</code>.</p>
<p class="files">writes nothing</p></li>

<li><h4>git switch -c feature/&lt;prefix&gt;/&lt;run-id&gt; <span class="pill auto">launcher only</span></h4>
<p>Creates the feature branch so later integration fast-forwards that branch, never <code>main</code>.</p></li>

<li><h4>prepare <span class="pill auto">no agents</span></h4>
<p><code>sessions.prepare()</code> resolves HEAD to a full SHA, checks the pinned commit contains the workflow contract, creates the run directory (mode 0700, must be outside the repo), and adds <code>worktree-ui</code> and <code>worktree-adapter</code> detached at that SHA. It journals <code>plan.json</code> before each allocation so a crash leaves an inspectable partial plan. Each worker's task text gets the JSON of its own policy entry appended so the worker sees its owned paths and checks. Then <code>run-state.json</code> is written for the first time.</p>
<p class="files">plan.json · policy.json · worktree-ui/ · worktree-adapter/ · run-state.json</p></li>

<li><h4>start --live [--herdr] <span class="pill auto">launches 2 Claude sessions</span></h4>
<p>First <code>graph.invoke({"run_id": …})</code>. Superstep 1 runs <code>launch_ui</code> and <code>launch_adapter</code>, each calling <code>InteractiveSessions.run()</code>. The graph then enters <code>handoff</code>, hits <code>interrupt()</code>, and the process exits printing the report path. With <code>--herdr</code> it also creates a tab with two panes attached to the sessions.</p>
<p class="files">ui.interactive.json · adapter.interactive.json · ui.launch.log · adapter.launch.log · events.jsonl · pipeline.sqlite · terminals.json · report.html</p></li>

<li><h4>(human works in the terminals)</h4>
<p>No controller process is running. Idle is not completion. The operator asks each worker for a summary and open assumptions and saves those as two small JSON files.</p></li>

<li><h4>freeze --ui-handoff … --adapter-handoff … <span class="pill gate">human gate 1</span></h4>
<p>Requires <code>pending == ["worker_handoff"]</code>. Copies the handoffs into the run, then resumes with <code>{"freeze": true}</code>. The handoff node validates both handoffs, stops both native sessions, snapshots both worktrees (section 8), and returns <code>snapshots</code>. The graph immediately continues into superstep 3, running both verification lanes (section 9), then <code>candidate</code>, then stops at the <code>review</code> interrupt. If any lane is blocked the node raises and the CLI exits 1 with "Blocked: …".</p>
<p class="files">ui.handoff.json · ui.stop.json · snapshots.json · verification/worker/&lt;node&gt;/1/ · candidate/ · candidate.json · verification/candidate/&lt;node&gt;/1/ · review-bundle.json</p></li>

<li><h4>retry [--phase worker|candidate --node ui|adapter]</h4>
<p>Only allowed when a step failed (no interrupt pending, <code>state.next</code> non-empty, no launch step pending). With <code>--node</code>, bumps that lane's attempt counter through <code>retry_check()</code>, which enforces <code>max_verification_attempts</code>. Then <code>invoke(None)</code> continues the checkpoint. Successful sibling lanes are reused; a lane whose packet already exists on disk is re-validated rather than rerun.</p>
<p class="files">attempts.json · verification/&lt;phase&gt;/&lt;node&gt;/2/</p></li>

<li><h4>review --review-file … <span class="pill gate">human gate 2</span></h4>
<p>Requires <code>pending == ["independent_review"]</code>. The review JSON must carry this run id, the SHA-256 of <code>review-bundle.json</code> as it exists now, the candidate commit, a reviewer identity that is not one of the two worker session ids, <code>independent: true</code>, verdict <code>approved</code>, and no unresolved P0/P1 findings. Validated twice: in the CLI before resuming, and inside the node after resuming.</p>
<p class="files">review.json</p></li>

<li><h4>approve --bundle-sha256 … <span class="pill gate">human gate 3</span></h4>
<p>Requires <code>pending == ["integration_approval"]</code> and the exact digest. Resuming with <code>{"approve": digest}</code> runs <code>integrate</code>: re-validate the bundle and review, require the source checkout to be on the pinned branch, clean, and still at the base commit, write <code>integration-intent.json</code>, then <code>git merge --ff-only &lt;candidate&gt;</code>. No push.</p>
<p class="files">integration-intent.json · report.html</p></li>

<li><h4>status</h4>
<p>Read-only: prints <code>next</code>, pending interrupt kinds, task errors, and rewrites <code>report.html</code> and <code>run-state.json</code>.</p></li>
</ol>

<h2 id="rundir">6. The run directory is the truth</h2>
<p>Nothing important lives only in memory or only in the checkpoint database. The checkpoint records where the graph is; the JSON files record what was done and are what every validation re-reads. All JSON writes go through <code>save_json</code>: write to a temp file with mode 0600, fsync, rename over the target, fsync the directory. A reader never sees a half-written file.</p>

