<h2 id="sessions">7. Worker sessions: launch, bind, attach, stop</h2>
<p>A worker is a native Claude background terminal, not a subprocess the controller owns. The controller's problem is therefore identity: how to know, in a later process, which terminal is "the ui worker" without guessing.</p>

<h3>Launch</h3>
<p><code>InteractiveSessions.run(node)</code> first writes a receipt with <code>status: launching</code>, so a crash after this point can never lead to a blind relaunch. It refuses if the worktree is not clean at the base commit, or if any session already has the launch name <code>workflow-&lt;run&gt;-&lt;node&gt;</code>. It then runs:</p>
<pre><code>claude --bg --name workflow-&lt;run&gt;-&lt;node&gt; --safe-mode --strict-mcp-config \
       --mcp-config '{"mcpServers":{}}' --tools Read,Glob,Grep,Edit,Write \
       --permission-mode manual "&lt;system framing + task text&gt;"</code></pre>
<p>That helper command creates Claude's own persistent terminal and exits. Its stdout is kept in <code>&lt;node&gt;.launch.log</code>. Note what is deliberately absent: no shell tool, no MCP servers, no nested agents, and every edit needs manual permission from the human in the pane.</p>

<h3>Bind and locate</h3>
<p><code>locate(node, rows)</code> takes the output of <code>claude agents --json</code> and finds the one row whose <code>id</code> equals the receipt's background id (or, on the very first pass, the single <code>claude attach &lt;id&gt;</code> line printed in the launch log). It then requires: kind <code>background</code>, cwd equal to the node's worktree, the expected name, an unchanged native session UUID, an attachable state, and a live pid that answers <code>kill(pid, 0)</code>. Any mismatch raises rather than returning "probably this one". If a receipt exists, <code>run()</code> only ever reconciles through <code>locate()</code>; it never launches again.</p>

<h3>Attach</h3>
<p><code>attach_panels()</code> creates a Herdr tab with two panes and types <code>python -m workflow.interactive attach-one &lt;run&gt; --node ui</code> into each. That entry point re-runs <code>locate()</code> in the pane process itself and then <code>execvp("claude attach &lt;id&gt;")</code>, so the pane is attached to a verified session, not to a pane id remembered from earlier. It refuses to type into a pane that already has a foreground process.</p>

<h3>Stop</h3>
<p><code>stop_workers()</code> runs at freeze. For each node it writes a stop intent naming the background id, session UUID and pid, re-checks the inventory still shows exactly that identity, runs <code>claude stop &lt;id&gt;</code>, then confirms the session is gone from the inventory and the pid is dead before marking <code>stopped: true</code>. If the process crashes between the stop and the confirmation, the next freeze reads the intent and only confirms; it never stops twice. Finally it checks none of the stopped session ids has reappeared.</p>

<h2 id="git">8. Git: snapshots, candidate, integration</h2>
<p>The worker worktrees are never committed to. Instead freeze builds commits from their contents using a private index, so the worker's own index and HEAD are untouched and a worker cannot pre-shape what gets captured.</p>

