<p>Two checks make the snapshot trustworthy. Before capture, <code>changed_files()</code> lists tracked and untracked changes and each path must be a safe repository-relative path inside one of the worker's <code>owned_paths</code>. After capture, the file list of the written tree against the base is compared to that list; a difference means files changed during the snapshot, and the freeze is refused. If a worker changed nothing, its snapshot commit is simply the base commit. Worker HEADs are also required to still be the base commit, so a worker that committed on its own is reported rather than silently accepted.</p>

<h2 id="verify">9. Verification lanes and the evidence gate</h2>
<p>A lane is one call to <code>verify_revision(run, plan, policy, node, commit, changed, session_id, phase, attempt)</code>. The phase is <code>worker</code> (the node's own snapshot) or <code>candidate</code> (the combined commit); the checks run are always the ones listed for that node in the policy. In order:</p>
<ol>
<li>If <code>packet.json</code> already exists for this lane and attempt, verify it belongs to the same commit and policy digest, re-evaluate it, and return. Never rerun a completed lane.</li>
<li>If the lane directory exists without a packet, the previous attempt was interrupted. Refuse; the operator must bump the attempt.</li>
<li>Create the directory with mode 0700, <code>git worktree add --detach</code> at the commit, and assert HEAD matches and the tree is clean.</li>
<li>Build an environment: drop every <code>HERDR_*</code> and inherited <code>PLAYWRIGHT_JSON_OUTPUT*</code> variable; prepend the controller's Python to <code>PATH</code>; set <code>CI=1</code>, <code>NO_COLOR=1</code>, <code>WORKFLOW_VERIFICATION_PHASE=&lt;phase&gt;</code>; give the lane its own <code>TMPDIR</code>, <code>XDG_CACHE_HOME</code> and <code>npm_config_cache</code> directories. Only installed browser binaries are shared, read-only.</li>
<li>Run each policy <code>setup</code> command (for the Projects feature, <code>npm ci</code>), stopping at the first failure.</li>
<li>Run each check with <code>execute()</code>: a new process group, stdin closed, stdout and stderr into a log file, a hard timeout, and after exit a <code>SIGTERM</code> then <code>SIGKILL</code> to the whole group so no test server outlives its check. Browser checks get <code>--reporter=json --output=… --workers=1 --retries=0</code> appended and <code>PLAYWRIGHT_JSON_OUTPUT_FILE</code> pointed at a lane-private file; the approved argv is recorded as the command and the appended flags separately as <code>effective_commands</code>.</li>
<li>Extract test counts. Python <code>unittest</code> output and Node's TAP summary are parsed; anything else yields <code>tests: null</code>, which the gate treats as "no evidence". For browser checks, <code>browser_evidence()</code> walks the Playwright JSON, requires every scenario id from the policy to appear in exactly one test title as <code>[scenario:&lt;id&gt;]</code>, counts a test as passed only if it ran once with no retry and expected status passed, and copies the <code>screenshot:&lt;id&gt;</code> attachment from the lane's own output directory.</li>
<li>Every log, report and screenshot goes through <code>Capture.add()</code>: copied into <code>artifacts/</code> with an id containing its SHA-256 prefix, then <code>chmod 0400</code>.</li>
<li>Assert the worktree is still clean at the commit; a check that edited source invalidates the lane.</li>
<li>Assemble the packet: <code>expected</code> (backend-owned identity), <code>result</code> (a contract-shaped <code>workerResult</code>), <code>evidence</code> (per-check receipts), the artifact registry, and <code>capture_errors</code>. Run <code>recheck_packet()</code> and save.</li>
</ol>

<h3>The gate</h3>
<p><code>evaluate_worker()</code> in <code>verification.py</code> is a pure function that returns <code>passed</code> or <code>blocked</code> with reasons. It validates both payloads against the JSON schemas, requires every identity field to match <code>expected</code>, requires the policy digest to match, requires a log artifact per executed check, chronological timestamps, exit code 0, the exact approved argv, the right worktree, execution within the approved timeout, at least one passing test and zero failures for unit/browser/contract/integration checks, one passed scenario per required browser scenario each with a distinct PNG whose header parses, and, in the worker phase, every changed file inside the owned paths. Any exception during evaluation becomes a reason. There is no way to get <code>passed</code> with a missing input.</p>

<div class="callout">
<p><strong>Rechecked on every read.</strong> <code>recheck_packet()</code> re-runs the gate and re-hashes every artifact whenever a packet is loaded: at candidate time, in <code>validate_bundle()</code> before review, again before approval, and again inside <code>integrate()</code>. Tampering with an artifact after the fact turns a passed lane into a blocked one, which the tests demonstrate by overwriting a screenshot.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="recovery">10. Recovery: retries, drills, reconcile</h2>
<p>Every failure is a raised exception inside a node. The CLI catches it, prints "Blocked: …", regenerates the report, and exits 1 with everything on disk. The next CLI process decides what is allowed from <code>state.next</code> and the pending interrupt kinds.</p>

