<h3>Attempt limits</h3>
<p><code>attempt(phase, node)</code> reads the counter and raises if it is above <code>max_verification_attempts</code> (default 3). <code>retry_check()</code> refuses to increment past it. The counter is bumped before the graph is invoked, so a retry that fails again has still consumed an attempt. Reaching the limit means "start a revised run"; there is no reset.</p>

<h3>The lab drill</h3>
<p>Policy v1.1.0 can set <code>failure_drill: {node_id, phase: worker, attempt: 1}</code>. When set, <code>verify()</code> runs the real checks for that node's first attempt and then marks the packet blocked with an explicit reason string, saving <code>failure-drill.json</code> with the launch receipts as they stood. The operator must retry as above. At candidate time the receipts are compared again and <code>failure-report.json</code> lists which lanes ran which attempts. The point is to rehearse the reopen-checkpoint path on a real run without a real failure; the packet's reason text says so.</p>

<h3>Reconcile</h3>
<p>If a launch node failed, for example because <code>claude agents --json</code> could not be read after the terminal was created, the receipt is left at <code>needs_reconciliation</code>. <code>reconcile</code> is only allowed when launch steps are pending and a receipt exists; it calls <code>sessions.run()</code>, which takes the existing-receipt branch and can only locate, never launch. Missing proof stays blocked.</p>

<h3>Failure inside a resumed node</h3>
<p>If <code>freeze()</code> raises after the handoff resume (for example an ownership violation), LangGraph keeps both the interrupt and the error on the task and empties <code>state.next</code>. The correct operator move is to fix the cause and run <code>freeze</code> again; it re-resumes, and the node reruns from its first line. <code>retry</code> is refused in that state because an interrupt is pending.</p>

<h2 id="export">11. The export seam for the viewer</h2>
<p><code>export_state()</code> is the only bridge to the upcoming Projects viewer. It writes <code>run-state.json</code> containing the run identity, a pinned graph definition (<code>GRAPH_NODES</code> with labels, kinds and <code>depends_on</code>), the raw LangGraph <code>values</code>, <code>next</code>, per-task errors and interrupt payloads, the event log, and a list of every packet with its relative path and SHA-256. It compares against the previous export and rewrites only when something other than <code>updated_at</code> changed, so <code>updated_at</code> means "state changed", not "someone ran status". The adapter is told to read this file and nothing else, to resolve packets only through the listed relative paths with hash checks, and never to expose <code>values</code> directly because it contains absolute paths.</p>

<h2 id="tests">12. How the tests prove it offline</h2>
<p>The suite runs the real graph end to end with no model calls. <code>test_pipeline.py</code> builds a throwaway Git repository containing a text file, a Python module with a <code>unittest</code>, a tiny Playwright spec that screenshots a heading, and a policy with build, browser and unit checks. <code>FakeSessions</code> replaces <code>InteractiveSessions</code>: its <code>run()</code> writes a file into the worktree and drops a handoff, and <code>OfflinePipeline</code> overrides <code>stop_workers()</code> to skip the native stop. Everything else is real: worktrees, private-index snapshots, headless Chromium, artifact hashing, cherry-picks, and a genuine <code>merge --ff-only</code> on the temp repo. The recovery test injects a one-time unit failure via a marker file, asserts the ui packet's hash is unchanged after retry, and that no second ui attempt directory exists. The drill test does the same through the policy and checks the attempt cap. Native stop paths are tested against a mocked inventory, and <code>test_feature_launch.py</code> checks the committed feature policy validates and that the launcher runs nothing without <code>--live</code>.</p>
